/-
# The Carlson-Thévenaz conjecture on endo-trivial modules (Oberwolfach Report 24/2015)

J. Thévenaz (joint work with J. F. Carlson), Torsion endo-trivial modules, Oberwolfach Report
24/2015, p. 1365 ff., Conjecture: let `S` be a Sylow `p`-subgroup of the finite group `G`, put
`ρ₁(Q) = N_G(Q)'` (the commutator subgroup of `N_G(Q)` times a Sylow `p`-subgroup of `N_G(Q)`),
`ρ_i(Q) = ⟨N_G(Q) ∩ ρ_{i-1}(R) | 1 ≠ R ⊆ S⟩` and `ρ^∞(S) = ⋃ ρ_i(S)`; (a) a one-dimensional
`kN_G(S)`-module `L` satisfies `M↓_{N_G(S)} ≅ L ⊕ (proj)` for some endo-trivial `kG`-module `M` if
and only if `ρ^∞(S)` is in the kernel of `L`; (b) the image of `K(G) → K(N_G(S))` is the dual of
`N_G(S)/ρ^∞(S)`. The report notes that (b) is an immediate consequence of (a).
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.FieldTheory.IsAlgClosed.Basic
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.Sylow
public import Mathlib.Order.CompletePartialOrder
public import Mathlib.RepresentationTheory.Rep.Res

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.CarlsonThevenazConjectureOWR13681Problem015Wanted

/-!
Source: J. Thévenaz (joint work with J. F. Carlson), Torsion endo-trivial modules, in Cohomology of
Finite Groups: Interactions and Applications, Oberwolfach Reports 12 (2015), no. 24, p. 1365 ff.,
Conjecture, DOI 10.4171/OWR/2015/24. UnsolvedMath record UM-30002881 (`OWR-13681-015`), dataset
ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Modules are Mathlib `Rep k G` objects; `End_k(M)` with the conjugation action is the internal hom
`(ihom M).obj M`, `(proj)` is a projective object, `↓` is `Rep.res` along the inclusion of `N_G(S)`,
and the one-dimensional modules are the modules `L_χ` of characters `χ : N_G(S) →* kˣ` (every
one-dimensional representation is one), with kernel `ker χ`. `N_G(Q)'` is encoded as the subgroup
generated by the commutators and the `p`-elements of `N_G(Q)` (`O^{p'}(N_G(Q))[N_G(Q), N_G(Q)]`,
Grodal's `A_{p'}(N_G(Q))`), which equals the commutator subgroup times any Sylow `p`-subgroup. `S`
is assumed non-trivial (`p` divides `|G|`), as in Grodal's Theorem F; `k` is an algebraically closed
field of characteristic `p`, the report's standing assumption (Grodal proves the statement for every
field of characteristic `p`; the original conjecture had the algebraically closed assumption, as his
Section 1.3 notes). Endo-triviality uses `End_k(M) = Hom_k(M, M)`, which is the report's `M ⊗ M*`
for finite-dimensional `M`. Part (b) is not stated separately: the report records that it follows
immediately from (a).
-/

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

/-- `A_{p'}(N) = O^{p'}(N) [N, N]` for a subgroup `N` of `G`: the subgroup generated by the
commutators of elements of `N` and by the `p`-elements of `N`. It is the smallest normal subgroup of
`N` with abelian `p'`-quotient, equivalently the report's `N'`, the product of the commutator
subgroup of `N` and a Sylow `p`-subgroup of `N` (all Sylow `p`-subgroups of `N` have the same image
in the abelianization). -/
def abelianPPrimeResidual {G : Type} [Group G] (p : ℕ) (N : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  ⁅N, N⁆ ⊔ Subgroup.closure {g | g ∈ N ∧ ∃ n : ℕ, g ^ (p ^ n) = 1}

/-- The Carlson-Thévenaz subgroups `ρ_{i+1}(Q)` for a Sylow `p`-subgroup `S` (the index `i` stands
for `ρ_{i+1}`): `ρ₁(Q) = A_{p'}(N_G(Q))` and `ρ_{i+1}(Q) = ⟨N_G(Q) ∩ ρ_i(R) | 1 ≠ R ≤ S⟩`. They are
used for non-trivial subgroups `Q ≤ S`. -/
def rho {G : Type} [Group G] (p : ℕ) (S : Subgroup G) : ℕ → Subgroup G → Subgroup G
  | 0, Q => abelianPPrimeResidual p (Subgroup.normalizer (Q : Set G))
  | i + 1, Q =>
    ⨆ (R : Subgroup G) (_ : R ≤ S) (_ : R ≠ ⊥), Subgroup.normalizer (Q : Set G) ⊓ rho p S i R

/-- `ρ^∞(S)`, the union (here the supremum) of the increasing chain `ρ₁(S) ⊆ ρ₂(S) ⊆ ⋯`. -/
def rhoInfty {G : Type} [Group G] (p : ℕ) (S : Subgroup G) : Subgroup G :=
  ⨆ i : ℕ, rho p S i S

/-- The one-dimensional `kN`-module `L` on which `g ∈ N` acts by the scalar `χ g`. -/
noncomputable def characterRep {k N : Type} [Field k] [Group N] (χ : N →* kˣ) : Rep k N :=
  Rep.of ((Algebra.lsmul k k k).toMonoidHom.comp ((Units.coeHom k).comp χ))

/-- A `kG`-module `M` is endo-trivial if it is finite-dimensional and `End_k(M) = Hom_k(M, M)`, with
`G` acting by conjugation (the internal hom of `Rep k G`; for finite-dimensional `M` it is the
report's `M ⊗ M*`), is isomorphic to the trivial module `k` direct sum a projective `kG`-module. -/
def IsEndotrivial {k G : Type} [Field k] [Group G] (M : Rep k G) : Prop :=
  FiniteDimensional k M ∧
    ∃ P : Rep k G, Projective P ∧ Nonempty ((ihom M).obj M ≅ Rep.trivial k G k ⊞ P)

/-- The Carlson-Thévenaz conjecture, part (a) (part (b) is its reformulation): let `G` be a finite
group, `k` an algebraically closed field of characteristic `p`, `S` a non-trivial Sylow
`p`-subgroup of `G` and `L` the one-dimensional `kN_G(S)`-module of a character `χ`. Then there is
an endo-trivial `kG`-module `M` with `M↓_{N_G(S)} ≅ L ⊕ (proj)` if and only if `ρ^∞(S)` is
contained in the kernel of `L`. -/
def carlsonThevenazConjecture : Prop :=
  ∀ (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G] (k : Type) [Field k] [IsAlgClosed k]
    [CharP k p] (S : Sylow p G), (S : Subgroup G) ≠ ⊥ →
    ∀ χ : Subgroup.normalizer ((S : Subgroup G) : Set G) →* kˣ,
      (∃ M : Rep k G, IsEndotrivial M ∧
        ∃ P : Rep k (Subgroup.normalizer ((S : Subgroup G) : Set G)), Projective P ∧
          Nonempty (Rep.res (Subgroup.normalizer ((S : Subgroup G) : Set G)).subtype M ≅
            characterRep χ ⊞ P)) ↔
        rhoInfty p (S : Subgroup G) ≤
          χ.ker.map (Subgroup.normalizer ((S : Subgroup G) : Set G)).subtype

/--
Resolved true: Grodal's Theorem F ("The Carlson-Thévenaz conjecture"): for a finite group `G` with
non-trivial Sylow `p`-subgroup `S`, `ρ₁(Q) = A_{p'}(N_G(Q))`, `ρ_i(Q) = ⟨N_G(Q) ∩ ρ_{i-1}(R) | 1 < R
≤ S⟩`, and any field `k` of characteristic `p` (in particular every algebraically closed one, the
setting of the report), `T_k(G, S) ≅ Hom(N_G(S)/ρ_r(S), kˣ)` for all large `r`, where `T_k(G, S)`
(the endo-trivial modules that restrict to `k ⊕ (proj)` on `S`) embeds into `Hom(N_G(S), kˣ)` by
restriction to `N_G(S)` (his Proposition 3.2 and Theorem 3.11); the paper states that this
strengthens the conjecture. Source: J. Thévenaz (joint work with J. F. Carlson), Torsion
endo-trivial modules, in Cohomology of Finite Groups: Interactions and Applications, Oberwolfach
Reports 12 (2015), no. 24, p. 1365 ff., Conjecture, DOI 10.4171/OWR/2015/24; resolution: J. Grodal,
Endotrivial modules for finite groups via homotopy theory, J. Amer. Math. Soc. 36 (2023), no. 1,
177-250, DOI 10.1090/jams/994, arXiv:1608.00499, Theorem F (The Carlson-Thévenaz conjecture [CT15,
Ques. 5.5]); Theorem A; Proposition 3.2 (restriction gives T_k(G, S) ↪ T_k(N_G(S), S) ≅
Hom(N_G(S)/S, kˣ)); Theorem 3.11, https://arxiv.org/abs/1608.00499.
-/
public theorem_wanted carlsonThevenazConjecture_holds : carlsonThevenazConjecture

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.CarlsonThevenazConjectureOWR13681Problem015Wanted
